  "new_theory_whole_utf8": "\\documentclass[11pt]{article}\n\\usepackage{amsmath,amssymb,amsthm}\n\\newtheorem{theorem}{Theorem}\n\\newtheorem{lemma}[theorem]{Lemma}\n\\newtheorem{corollary}[theorem]{Corollary}\n\\newcommand{\\F}{\\mathbf F}\n\\newcommand{\\SO}{\\operatorname{SO}}\n\\newcommand{\\Aut}{\\operatorname{Aut}}\n\\title{A fixed-line extension of the truncated dihedral star algebra}\n\\author{Conditional mathematical review: /root/readback\\_review}\n\\date{}\n\\begin{document}\\maketitle\n\nThis is an elementary conditional algebra argument. It does not assert any\nnew relation, rank, commutant dimension, tensor equation, or Original case\nresult. It replaces the independent six-element truncated-dihedral basis\nhypothesis by a different six-element basis containing a fixed-line\nidempotent. The earlier basis and the basis below must not be conflated.\nThe old numerical roles and their certified identities are imported, not\nrepeated. No classification theorem is used in this argument.\n\n\\section{Hypotheses and the nilpotent ideal}\nLet $V=\\F_{11}^{\\,7}$ and let $Q$ be a nonsingular symmetric matrix. Matrices\nact on row vectors on the right, and the adjoint is\n\\[\n X^*=QX^{\\mathsf T}Q^{-1}.\n\\]\nLet $C\\subseteq\\operatorname{End}_{\\F_{11}}(V)$ be a unital, $*$-stable\nalgebra. Assume that $I,B,N,BN,N^2,E$ is a basis of $C$, and that\n\\begin{align*}\n B^2&=I,& BN&=-NB,& N^3&=0,& N^2&\\ne0,& BN^2&=-N^2,\\\\\n B^*&=B,&N^*&=-N,&E^*&=E,&E^2&=E,&\\operatorname{rank}E&=1,\\\\\n BE&=EB=E,& EN&=NE=0,&\\det B&=1.\n\\end{align*}\nIn particular, the relation $BN^2=-N^2$ is an explicit hypothesis, rather\nthan a conclusion inferred from a failed independent-basis test. Put\n\\[\n J=\\langle N,BN,N^2\\rangle,\\qquad\n e=E,\\quad p=\\frac{I+B}{2}-E,\\quad q=\\frac{I-B}{2}.\n\\]\n\n\\begin{lemma}\\label{ideal}\nThe subspace $J$ is a two-sided, $*$-stable ideal, $J^3=0$, and\n$J^-:=\\{z\\in J:z^*=-z\\}=\\langle N\\rangle$.\nThe matrices $e,p,q$ are pairwise orthogonal self-adjoint idempotents whose\nsum is $I$. Their images form the three standard idempotents of\n$C/J\\simeq\\F_{11}^{\\,3}$, and the induced involution on this quotient is\nthe identity.\n\\end{lemma}\n\\begin{proof}\nMultiplication on either side by $B$ carries $N,BN,N^2$ into their span;\nhere $N^2B=BN^2=-N^2$. Multiplication by $N$ or $BN$ carries $J$ into\n$\\langle N^2\\rangle$, and multiplication by $E$ kills $J$. Thus $J$ is\nan ideal, $J^2\\subseteq\\langle N^2\\rangle$, and $J^3=0$.\nMoreover $(BN)^*=BN$ and $(N^2)^*=N^2$, while $N^*=-N$.\nThe basis hypothesis and characteristic $11$ therefore give $J^-=\\langle N\\rangle$.\nThe relations for $E$ show that it is central in $C$; direct multiplication\ngives all the stated idempotent identities. Modulo $J$, the three independent\nclasses $I,B,E$ span the quotient. The change of basis from them to $e,p,q$\nis invertible, so these are three nonzero orthogonal quotient idempotents.\nAll are self-adjoint, proving the quotient description.\n\\end{proof}\n\n\\section{All norm-one and determinant-one elements}\nWrite\n\\[\n U(t)=I+tN+\\frac{t^2}{2}N^2,\\qquad U=U(1),\\qquad \\sigma=2E-I.\n\\]\n\n\\begin{theorem}\\label{units}\nUnder the hypotheses above, the norm-one elements of $C$ are exactly\n\\[\n \\bigl\\{(s_e e+s_p p+s_q q)U(t):\n       s_e,s_p,s_q\\in\\{1,-1\\},\\ t\\in\\F_{11}\\bigr\\}.\n\\]\nThey are $88$ distinct elements. Their determinant-one subgroup is\n\\[\n \\SO(Q)\\cap C=\\langle B,U\\rangle\\times\\langle\\sigma\\rangle,\n \\qquad |\\SO(Q)\\cap C|=44,\n\\]\nwhere $\\langle B,U\\rangle$ is dihedral of order $22$ and $\\sigma$ is a\ncentral involution outside that group.\n\\end{theorem}\n\\begin{proof}\nFor a square matrix, $x^*x=I$ implies invertibility and $xx^*=I$, so either\nnorm convention is equivalent. Reduction modulo $J$ gives three independent\nequations $s_i^2=1$. Each of their eight solutions has the exact norm-one\nlift $a=s_e e+s_p p+s_q q$, with $a^*=a$ and $a^2=I$.\nIf $x$ is norm one with this quotient, then $v=ax$ lies in $I+J$ and is\nnorm one. Thus it remains to determine the norm-one kernel in $I+J$.\n\nFor $v\\in I+J$, the matrix $I+v=2I+j$ is invertible since $J$ is nilpotent.\nThe Cayley transform and its inverse are\n\\[\n z=(I-v)(I+v)^{-1},\\qquad v=(I-z)(I+z)^{-1}.\n\\]\nThese are inverse bijections between norm-one $v\\in I+J$ and skew-adjoint\n$z\\in J$. Indeed $v^*=v^{-1}$ implies $z^*=-z$, and the converse follows\nby the same identities; polynomials and inverses in the single matrix\nin question commute. This Cayley statement is used only on $I+J$, not on\narbitrary norm-one elements that may have eigenvalue $-1$.\nBy Lemma~\\ref{ideal}, $z=tN$. Since $N^3=0$, its inverse Cayley transform is\n\\[\n I-2tN+2t^2N^2=U(-2t).\n\\]\nConsequently the norm-one kernel is exactly $\\{U(t):t\\in\\F_{11}\\}$.\nThe product rule $U(s)U(t)=U(s+t)$ and independence of $N,N^2$ show that\nthese are eleven distinct elements, generated by $U$.\n\nBecause $B$ is an involution, $qV$ is its negative eigenspace.\nThe equation $\\det B=1$ makes $\\operatorname{rank}q$ even.\nThe orthogonal idempotent decomposition gives\n$\\operatorname{rank}p+\\operatorname{rank}q=6$, so\n$\\operatorname{rank}p$ is even as well. Therefore\n\\[\n \\det(s_e e+s_p p+s_q q)=s_e,\n\\]\nand each unipotent $U(t)$ has determinant one. Exactly the four quotient\nsigns with $s_e=1$ survive. They are represented by\n$I,B,\\sigma,B\\sigma$. Finally $BUB=U^{-1}$ and $B\\notin\\langle U\\rangle$\n(their quotient signs differ). Thus $D:=\\langle B,U\\rangle$ has order22.\nCentrality of $E$ gives centrality of $\\sigma$; its quotient has $p$-sign\n$-1$, whereas every element of $D$ has $p$-sign $+1$.\nIt is therefore outside $D$, proving the asserted direct product and count.\n\\end{proof}\n\n\\section{Excluding the extra coset with a three-form}\nLet $r_0,r_1,r_2,r_3$ be the exact same Original marks, and put\n$H=\\langle r_0,r_1,r_2\\rangle$, $R=\\langle r_1,r_2,r_3\\rangle$